          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 text-center mt-2">The southeast corner tower mirrored in the moat.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7: THREE THINGS ============ -->
    <hr class="border-gray-200 my-12" />
    <h2 id="skip" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🚫 Three Things You Should Not Skip</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-3 mb-12 text-lg text-gray-700 list-decimal pl-6 flow-root">
      <li><strong>The Hall of Supreme Harmony (太和殿)</strong> — the largest wooden structure in China. The throne sits in the middle. The bronze turtles and cranes in front of it were incense burners — the smoke was meant to purify the air symbolically before imperial audiences.</li>
      <li><strong>The Treasure Gallery (珍宝馆)</strong> — the ¥10 ticket is the best value-for-money of any museum in China. Hall of Supreme Harmony's original roof ridge beasts, the Qianlong-era gold and jade ceremonial weapons, the gold-leaf Buddhas brought back from Tibet in the 18th century. Allow 45 minutes minimum.
        <figure class="mt-4 md:float-right md:clear-right md:ml-8 md:w-[38%] not-prose">
          <img
            src="/images/forbidden-city/nine-dragon-wall.jpg"
            alt="Close-up of one of the glazed dragons on the Nine Dragon Wall inside the Forbidden City Treasure Gallery complex"
            width="442"
            height="300"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full h-auto object-cover rounded-2xl shadow-md"
          />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 text-center mt-2">A glazed dragon on the Nine Dragon Wall, inside the Treasure Gallery complex.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </li>

      <li><strong>The Palace of Heavenly Purity (乾清宫)</strong> — the emperors' residence through the Ming and into the early Qing. The central plaque above the throne (<strong>正大光明</strong>, "bright and upright") was written personally by each new Qing emperor. The "thirteen princes" plaques on either side are engraved instructions for imperial conduct.</li>
    </ol>

